var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Cards","page":"Cards","text":"A FITS header is a list of 80-character cards, or keyword records. A Card holds the parts of one card, the keyword, the value, the units, and the comment, and the layout that is needed to write the card as it was read. This page describes the creation, the parsing, and the modification of cards. The rules are those of the FITS Standard 4.0, Sect. 4, and of the ESO HIERARCH convention.\n\nThe examples use the following definitions, where show_ prints the 80-character image of a card without the trailing spaces.","title":"Cards"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Creating-a-card","page":"Cards","text":"A card is created from a keyword, a value, and a comment, which are all optional.\n\njulia> card = Card(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0, \"exposure time\");\n\njulia> show_(card)\nEXPTIME =               1200.0 / exposure time\n\njulia> card.key, card.value, card.units, card.comment\n(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0, nothing, \"exposure time\")\n\njulia> typeof(card)\nCard{Value{Float64}}\n\nThe type of the card is the kind of the card, Value, Hierarch, Comment, History, Continue, End, or Invalid, and for a value the type of the value.\n\nValue Card\nmissing, or none an undefined value\ntrue, false a logical\nInteger an integer\nAbstractFloat a real number\nComplex of integers or of floats a complex number\nAbstractString a string\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"NAXIS\", 2))\nNAXIS   =                    2\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"OBJECT\", \"NGC 1275\", \"target\"))\nOBJECT  = 'NGC 1275'           / target\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"FLAG\", true))\nFLAG    =                    T\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"Z\", 1.5 + 2.5im))\nZ       = (1.5, 2.5)\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"EMPTY\"))\nEMPTY   =\n\nOther values, e.g., a Rational or Complex{Bool}, are an ArgumentError, and a number that is not finite is a DomainError, since the FITS Standard has no such values. The text of a string and of a comment is the printable ASCII characters, 32 to 126.\n\nA keyword is converted to upper case. A keyword with the characters A-Z, 0-9, -, and _ and at most 8 characters is a regular keyword. A longer keyword is a HIERARCH keyword.","title":"Creating a card"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Keyword-arguments","page":"Cards","text":"The keyword arguments of Card format the card. The word keyword also means the name of a card, so a keyword argument is called a keyword here only if it is clear. The arguments are the layout of the card, and the layout of a card that is parsed is read from the image.\n\nKeyword Default Function\nfixed true Use the fixed format, where a number ends at byte 30 and a string begins at byte 11. The free format has the value anywhere after byte 10. The mandatory keywords are always in fixed format. See Fixed and free format.\nslash 32 The index, in bytes, of the comment separator /. A value that passes the index moves the separator and the comment to the right, and a separator at the end of the card has no comment, unless truncate = false, where the comment is continued at index 32. A number in fixed format ends at byte 30, so its separator is at byte 32 or later.\nlpad 1 The least number of spaces between the value and the comment separator.\nrpad 1 The number of spaces between the comment separator and the units or the comment.\nupad 1 The number of spaces between the units and the comment.\ntruncate true Truncate a comment that is longer than the space at the end of the card. If false, the comment of a string is continued on CONTINUE cards, and a comment of another value, and units, that do not fit are an ArgumentError. See Long strings.\nsplitat ' ' Split a long string and its comment after the last character splitat that fits, or at any character if nothing. See Long strings.\nappend false Append an ampersand to the string, which marks that a CONTINUE card continues the string. A string that is too long for a card has the ampersand without the keyword.\nwarn true Warn when a keyword with more than 8 characters creates a HIERARCH card.\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"KEY\", 1, \"comment\"))\nKEY     =                    1 / comment\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"KEY\", 1, \"comment\"; slash = 20, lpad = 0, rpad = 3))\nKEY     =                    1/   comment\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"KEY\", 1, \"comment\"; fixed = false))\nKEY     = 1                    / comment\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"KEY\", \"abc\", \"comment\"; append = true))\nKEY     = 'abc&'               / comment\n\nsetvalue! has the same keyword arguments, except warn, and the default of a layout keyword argument is the layout of the card, see Changing the value. The function parse has the keywords strict, which allows a lower case exponent letter if false, and units, which reads the units from the comment if true, see Parsing an image.","title":"Keyword arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Units","page":"Cards","text":"A Quantity is not a type of value. The units of a quantity are not part of the value, so a Quantity that is given in place of a number is split into its number and its units, and the type of the card is the type of the number, e.g., the type of a card with 1200.0u\"s\" is Card{Value{Float64}}. The units are read from the comment when a card is parsed, where the units are in square brackets at the beginning of the comment (Sect. 4.3.2), and they are read in the same way from the comment of a number that is given to Card: the unit string is removed from the comment and is the units of the card, as if the number were a Quantity.\n\njulia> card = Card(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0u\"s\", \"exposure time\");\n\njulia> show_(card)\nEXPTIME =               1200.0 / [s] exposure time\n\njulia> card.value, card.units\n(1200.0, s)\n\njulia> units(card)\ns\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"RATE\", 3.5f0u\"km/s\", \"velocity\"))\nRATE    =                  3.5 / [km s-1] velocity\n\njulia> card = Card(\"RATE\", 3.5f0, \"[km/s] velocity\"); card.units, card.comment\n(km s⁻¹, \"velocity\")\n\njulia> show_(card)\nRATE    =                  3.5 / [km/s] velocity\n\njulia> card = Card(\"RATE\", 3.5f0, \"[foo] velocity\"); card.units, card.comment\n(nothing, \"[foo] velocity\")\n\nThe style of the unit string of the comment is written again, e.g., [km/s], and a Quantity is written in the default style. The comment of a string or a logical, of a mandatory keyword, and of a number with a Quantity, is not units, and text that is not a unit string or a scale factor remains in the comment.\n\nThe units are written as the strings of the FITS Standard (Sect. 4.3), by formunit, and are read by parseunit. All the units of Tables 3 and 4 of the Standard are read, with the prefixes of Table 5 for the units that take them, e.g., a, the Julian year, Ma, R, the rayleigh, mag, D, the debye, and Sun. A unit with a scale factor, e.g., 10**3 m, or without a name in the Standard, e.g., a function such as log(Hz), remains in the comment. The units of a card are not read if the keyword units = false is given to parse.","title":"Units"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#DynamicQuantities","page":"Cards","text":"The units of a card are Unitful.jl units by default. The units can be DynamicQuantities.jl units instead, by the package extension DynamicQuantitiesExt, which is loaded when DynamicQuantities is loaded. It needs Julia 1.10 or later. The unit strings of the FITS Standard are the same for both packages. The examples are not run when the documentation is built, since DynamicQuantities is not a dependency of FITSFiles.","title":"DynamicQuantities"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Reading-units","page":"Cards","text":"The keyword units of parse(Card, ...), and of read for a Card or an HDU, is true for Unitful (the default), false for no units, or the module DynamicQuantities.\n\njulia> using FITSFiles, DynamicQuantities\n\njulia> image = rpad(\"RATE    =                  3.5 / [km/s] velocity\", 80);\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, image; units = DynamicQuantities);\n\njulia> card.value, card.units, card.comment\n(3.5f0, 1.0 s⁻¹ km, \"velocity\")\n\njulia> units(card)\n1.0 s⁻¹ km\n\njulia> card.value * units(card)\n3.5 s⁻¹ km\n\njulia> hdu = read(io, HDU; units = DynamicQuantities);   # the units of every number of the header\n\nA unit is a Quantity with the value one and symbolic dimensions, e.g., km/s has the symbols km and s, so the unit string of a card is written again from the symbols. The rules of the units of a card are those above: the units are in square brackets at the beginning of the comment of a number, a mandatory keyword has no units, and text that is not a unit string remains in the comment.","title":"Reading units"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Writing-units","page":"Cards","text":"A Quantity that is given in place of a number is split into its number and its units, as for Unitful.\n\njulia> card = Card(\"RATE\", 3.5 * DynamicQuantities.sym_uparse(\"km/s\"), \"velocity\");\n\njulia> println(card)\nRATE    =                  3.5 / [km s-1] velocity\n\njulia> card.units\n1.0 s⁻¹ km\n\nA quantity must have symbolic dimensions, e.g., from sym_uparse or us\"...\". A quantity that is expanded to the base units, e.g., by uexpand, has no symbols, so it has no FITS name and is an ArgumentError.","title":"Writing units"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Parsing-and-formatting-units","page":"Cards","text":"julia> using FITSFiles: parseunit, formunit\n\njulia> parseunit(DynamicQuantities, \"solMass/pc3\")\n1.0 pc⁻³ solMass\n\njulia> unit = parseunit(DynamicQuantities, \"erg/(cm2 s)\");\n\njulia> formunit(unit), formunit(unit, \" /d\")\n(\"erg s-1 cm-2\", \"erg/s/cm2\")\n\njulia> parseunit(DynamicQuantities, \"10**3 m\")      # a scale factor is a quantity and not a unit\n1000.0 m\n\njulia> parseunit(DynamicQuantities, \"foo\") === nothing\ntrue","title":"Parsing and formatting units"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Differences-from-Unitful","page":"Cards","text":"DynamicQuantities orders the units by its own symbol order, not by the order of the string, so kg m2 s-2 is written as m2 kg s-2.\nA unit that DynamicQuantities does not have is registered when the package is loaded, e.g., the units of Table 4 of the Standard such as solMass, Ry, lm, barn, D, R, and the units of counting (pix, ct, ph, adu, beam, chan, bin, voxel, bit, byte, mag, Sun). A prefixed unit, e.g., kyr or uJy, is registered the first time it is read. The units of counting have no dimension, but the symbols keep them apart, e.g., ct and ph.\nThe constants of DynamicQuantities that are units of the Standard, i.e., pc, au, ly, and u, and the gauss G, which DynamicQuantities calls Gauss, are read and written as the FITS names. a, the Julia year, is yr.\nThe registration uses DynamicQuantities.update_all_values, which is not public, so a future release of DynamicQuantities may change it.","title":"Differences from Unitful"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Fixed-and-free-format","page":"Cards","text":"The values of a card are in fixed format by default, where a number ends at byte 30 and a string begins at byte 11. A number has at most 20 characters in fixed format, so a Float64 that has more characters is written with fewer digits of the mantissa. The free format has no such limit.\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0, \"exposure time\"; fixed = false))\nEXPTIME = 1200.0               / exposure time\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"DELTA\", 1.2345678901234567e-100))\nDELTA   = 1.2345678901234D-100\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"DELTA\", 1.2345678901234567e-100; fixed = false))\nDELTA   = 1.2345678901234567D-100\n\nThe mandatory keywords SIMPLE, XTENSION, BITPIX, NAXIS, NAXISn, PCOUNT, GCOUNT, and END are always in fixed format and have no units.\n\nThe position of the comment is the keyword slash, the byte of the comment separator, which is 32 by default, and lpad, rpad, and upad are the numbers of spaces before and after the separator and after the units. A comment that is longer than the space at the end of the card is truncated, and the comment of the card is the truncated comment, unless truncate = false, which is an ArgumentError.\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0, \"exposure time\"; slash = 40))\nEXPTIME =               1200.0         / exposure time\n\njulia> Card(\"A\", 1, repeat(\"x\", 100)).comment |> length\n47\n\njulia> Card(\"A\", 1, repeat(\"x\", 100); truncate = false)\nERROR: ArgumentError: Comment does not fit on the card.\n[...]","title":"Fixed and free format"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Commentary-cards","page":"Cards","text":"A COMMENT, a HISTORY, and a blank keyword have no value indicator and only text.\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"COMMENT\", \"A comment\"))\nCOMMENT A comment\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"HISTORY\", \"processed\"))\nHISTORY processed\n\njulia> card = Card(\"COMMENT\", \"A comment\"); card.value\n\"A comment\"","title":"Commentary cards"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#HIERARCH-keywords","page":"Cards","text":"A keyword that is longer than 8 characters is a HIERARCH keyword, where the tokens of the keyword follow HIERARCH. A warning is given, unless warn = false. The key of the card is the tokens.\n\njulia> card = Card(\"HIERARCH\", \"ESO TEL FOCU SCALE\", 1.489, \"(deg/m) Focus\");\n\njulia> show_(card)\nHIERARCH ESO TEL FOCU SCALE = 1.489 / (deg/m) Focus\n\njulia> card.key, typeof(card)\n(\"ESO TEL FOCU SCALE\", Card{Hierarch{Float64}})\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"LONGKEYWORD\", 1.5; warn = false))\nHIERARCH LONGKEYWORD = 1.5\n\nThe tokens have the characters of a keyword, and a HIERARCH card is in free format. A card without a value, e.g., HIERARCH some text, is commentary text.","title":"HIERARCH keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Long-strings","page":"Cards","text":"A string of at most 68 characters, with the doubled single quotes, is on one card, and is not split. A string that passes the comment separator moves the separator and the comment to the right, and the comment is truncated if it does not fit on the card, unless truncate = false, where the comment is continued on CONTINUE cards.\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"DATE-OBS\", \"2020-01-01T00:00:00.000\", \"observation date\"))\nDATE-OBS= '2020-01-01T00:00:00.000' / observation date\n\njulia> card = Card(\"KEY\", repeat(\"x\", 60), \"a comment that is truncated\"); card.comment\n\"a com\"\n\njulia> cards = Card(\"KEY\", repeat(\"x\", 60), \"a comment that is continued\"; truncate = false);\n\njulia> foreach(show_, cards)\nKEY     = 'xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx&' / a\nCONTINUE  ''                   / comment that is continued\n\nThe spaces at the ends of a comment are not significant, so they are not part of the comment of a card. The comments of the cards of a long string are joined by joincards with a space, unless a comment ends with a space.\n\nA string that does not fit on one card is split into the cards of a long string, a card and the CONTINUE cards (Sect. 4.2.1.2), which are returned as a vector. Every card but the last ends with an ampersand. The string is split at the comment separator, or at the end of the card if there is no comment. A string is split after the last space that fits, unless the keyword splitat = nothing is given, where it is split at any character, or the keyword splitat is another character.\n\njulia> cards = Card(\"LONGSTR\", repeat(\"This is a long string value. \", 5), \"comment\");\n\njulia> foreach(show_, cards)\nLONGSTR = 'This is a long &'   / comment\nCONTINUE  'string value. &'\nCONTINUE  'This is a long &'\nCONTINUE  'string value. &'\nCONTINUE  'This is a long &'\nCONTINUE  'string value. &'\nCONTINUE  'This is a long &'\nCONTINUE  'string value. &'\nCONTINUE  'This is a long &'\nCONTINUE  'string value. '\n\nThe comment is split in the same way. A list of cards is joined into the value of the card by joincards.\n\njulia> joincards(cards).value\n\"This is a long string value. This is a long string value. This is a long string value. This is a long string value. This is a long string value. \"","title":"Long strings"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Parsing-an-image","page":"Cards","text":"parse(Card, image) creates a card from an 80-character image. The type of the card follows the image: a value, a HIERARCH keyword, commentary text, a CONTINUE card, an END card, or an invalid record.\n\njulia> image = rpad(\"EXPTIME = 1200. / [s] exposure time in seconds\", 80);\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, image);\n\njulia> card.key, card.value, card.units, card.comment\n(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0f0, s, \"exposure time in seconds\")\n\njulia> repr(card) == image\ntrue\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"COMMENT some text\", 80)))\nCard{Comment}\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"END\", 80)))\nCard{End}\n\nThe type of a number is the smallest type that holds the number, and a floating-point number is a Float32 only if no precision is lost, i.e., the Float32 is exactly the Float64 of its text, e.g., 0.5 is a Float32, but 0.1 and 266.4 are a Float64. An integer is an Int64, an Int128, or a BigInt.\n\nA record with a malformed value is an Invalid card, which keeps the text of the record and is written as it was read. Only a record that is not a value, e.g., a keyword without the value indicator = in bytes 9 and 10, is commentary text.\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY     = 1 2 3\", 80));\n\njulia> typeof(card), card.key\n(Card{Invalid}, \"KEY\")\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"HIERARCH ESO A = 1 2 3\", 80)))\nCard{Invalid}\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY=  1\", 80)))\nCard{Invalid}\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"HIERARCH some text\", 80)))\nCard{Comment}\n\nThe Standard allows an upper case E or D for the exponent. A number with a lower case letter is an invalid card, unless the keyword strict = false is given.\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY     = 1.5e3\", 80)))\nCard{Invalid}\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY     = 1.5e3\", 80); strict = false);\n\njulia> card.value\n1500.0f0\n\njulia> show_(card)\nKEY     = 1.5E3\n\nA CONTINUE card continues a string only if the card before it is a string that ends with an ampersand, so the card before it is given to parse. A CONTINUE card that does not continue a string, and a CONTINUE record without spaces in bytes 9 and 10 or without a string, is commentary.\n\njulia> images = [rpad(\"LONGSTR = 'This is a long &' / comment\", 80), rpad(\"CONTINUE  'string value.'\", 80)];\n\njulia> cards = Card{<:Any}[]; previous = nothing;\n\njulia> for image in images\n           card = parse(Card, image, previous)\n           push!(cards, card)\n           previous = card\n       end\n\njulia> typeof.(cards)\n2-element Vector{DataType}:\n Card{Value{String}}\n Card{Continue}\n\njulia> joincards(cards).value\n\"This is a long string value.\"\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"CONTINUE  'orphan'\", 80), nothing))\nCard{Comment}","title":"Parsing an image"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Reading-and-writing-a-card","page":"Cards","text":"A card is written as it was read, and the image of a parsed card is the image that was read, including the format of the numbers, the position of the values, the units, and the comment. The exceptions are those of the reading, where the value is not changed:\n\na number without an integer digit has a zero, e.g., .5 is 0.5;\nan exponent has at least two digits, and a mantissa has one integer digit, e.g., E+007 is E+07 and 56.53E+13 is 5.653E+14;\nthe sign of an integer zero is lost;\na number that is longer than before takes the spaces before the comment, but may move the comment, and the end of the comment of a full card is lost.\n\nTrailing spaces of a string are not significant, and are restored in the written card.","title":"Reading and writing a card"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Changing-the-value","page":"Cards","text":"setvalue! returns the card with a new value, and the units, format, and comment of the card. The new value may have another type. A number keeps the format of the old number, e.g., the digits and the exponent letter, only if the number is written with that format without a loss of digits. A number without units has the units of the card.\n\njulia> card = Card(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0, \"exposure time\");\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 1500.0))\nEXPTIME =               1500.0 / exposure time\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 1500.0u\"s\"))\nEXPTIME =               1500.0 / [s] exposure time\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(Card(\"N\", 1, \"c\"), \"text\"))\nN       = 'text'               / c\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY     = 1.50 / c\", 80));\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.5))\nKEY     = 2.50 / c\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.567))\nKEY     = 2.567 / c\n\nA string that does not fit is split into the cards of a long string, as by Card. The value of a commentary card is a string or missing, and an END card has no value, otherwise an ArgumentError is raised.\n\nThe keywords of setvalue! are the keywords of Card that format the card, and the default of a layout keyword is the layout of the card, so the new card is formatted as the card was.\n\nKeyword Default\nfixed the format of the card, fixed or free\nslash the index of the comment separator of the card, and for the cards of a long string the index 32 or more\nlpad 0 if the separator follows the value of the card directly, otherwise 1\nrpad the number of spaces after the separator of the card, or 1\nupad the number of spaces after the units of the card, or 1\nappend false\ntruncate true, as in Card\nsplitat ' ', as in Card\n\nA keyword formats the card again, even if the value is the same, and a keyword creates the same card as the constructor of a card with the keyword.\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY     = 1.5/ c\", 80));\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.5))\nKEY     = 2.5/ c\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.5; lpad = 3))\nKEY     = 2.5   / c\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.5; slash = 20))\nKEY     = 2.5      / c\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.5; fixed = true))\nKEY     =                  2.5/ c\n\nA list of cards in a header is changed by setvalue!(cards, key, value) and by cards[key] = value, which also replace the CONTINUE cards of the old value.","title":"Changing the value"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#A-list-of-cards","page":"Cards","text":"A header is a vector of cards, and the cards with a keyword are found by the keyword, without regard to the case.\n\njulia> cards = Card{<:Any}[Card(\"BITPIX\", 16), Card(\"BZERO\", 1.5), Card(\"OBJECT\", \"NGC 1275\"),\n                           Card(\"HISTORY\", \"first step\"), Card(\"HISTORY\", \"second step\")];\n\njulia> cards[\"bitpix\"], get(cards, \"NOPE\", 0), haskey(cards, \"OBJECT\")\n(16, 0, true)\n\njulia> get(cards, [\"BITPIX\", \"BZERO\", \"NOPE\"], [0, 0, \"default\"])\n(16, 1.5, \"default\")\n\njulia> FITSFiles.getall(cards, \"HISTORY\")\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"first step\"\n \"second step\"\n\njulia> findall(\"HISTORY\", cards)\n2-element Vector{Int64}:\n 4\n 5\n\nThe value of a card is the first card with the keyword, for getindex, get, and haskey, and findfirst is the index of the first card. With a list of keys, get is the tuple of the values of the first cards, where the types of the values are the types of the cards and not of the defaults. findall and getall are the indexes and the values of all the cards with a keyword, e.g., the COMMENT and HISTORY cards. The value of a continued string is the joined string, and setvalue!(cards, key, value) replaces the card and the CONTINUE cards of the old string.","title":"A list of cards"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Comparing-and-copying","page":"Cards","text":"Two cards are equal if their images are equal, so a card is equal to the card that is parsed from its image, and equal cards have the same hash. A copy has its own format, so the copy can be changed without changing the card.\n\njulia> a, b = Card(\"A\", 1, \"c\"), Card(\"A\", 1, \"c\");\n\njulia> a == b, hash(a) == hash(b), copy(a) == a, copy(a) === a\n(true, true, true, false)\n\njulia> parse(Card, repr(a)) == a\ntrue\n\nA card is a scalar in a broadcast, e.g., cards .== a.","title":"Comparing and copying"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Errors","page":"Cards","text":"Error Cause\nArgumentError a keyword, a string, or a comment with characters that are not allowed\nArgumentError a value of a type that cannot be written\nArgumentError a string value for a number, or a value that is not a string for a commentary or CONTINUE card\nArgumentError a comment, units, or a HIERARCH keyword and value that do not fit on the card\nDomainError a number that is not finite\n\nReading an image never raises an error. A record that is not valid is an Invalid card.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"#FITSFiles.jl","page":"FITSFiles","text":"FITSFiles.jl is a Julia implementation of the Flexible Image Transport System (FITS) file IO standard.\n\nA FITS file is a vector of header-data units (HDUs), where the header of an HDU is a vector of cards, and fits reads the file.","title":"FITSFiles.jl"},{"category":"module","location":"#FITSFiles.FITSFiles","page":"FITSFiles","text":"FITSFiles.jl is a Julia implementation of the Flexible Image Transport System (FITS) file format.\n\nFITS files are composed of a vector of header-data units (HDUs), where an HDU is a header, which is a vector of cards, followed by data.\n\nfits is the most common function for reading a file, and write writes it.\nHDU creates a header-data unit.\nCard creates, and parse reads, a card of a header.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.FITSFiles"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.getindex-Union{Tuple{U}, Tuple{AbstractVector{<:HDU}, U, Integer}} where U<:Union{AbstractString, Symbol}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"hdus[name]\nhdus[name, version]\n\nReturn the HDU of the vector hdus whose EXTNAME is name, without regard to the case, which is the first HDU of that name if the name is not unique. With a version, return the HDU whose EXTVER is version, where an absent EXTVER is 1 (Sect. 4.4.2.6 of the standard). A KeyError is raised if there is no such HDU.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.write-Tuple{IO, AbstractVector{<:HDU}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.write(io::IO, hdus::AbstractVector{<:HDU})\nBase.write(filename::AbstractString, hdus::AbstractVector{<:HDU})\n\nWrite a vector of header-data units (HDUs), of any type of HDU, e.g., the Vector{HDU{Primary}} of [HDU(data, cards)], to a file, with a warning if the first HDU is not a primary HDU or a later one is (Sect. 3.1 of the standard). The keyword checksum::Bool=false sets the DATASUM and CHECKSUM keywords of each HDU (see Base.write(io::IO, hdu::HDU)). The data of the HDUs of a file that are not read are copied from the file, so the vector is not written to that file, which is an ArgumentError that leaves the file as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.write"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.fits-Tuple{IO}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"fits(io::IO; <keywords>)\nfits(filename::AbstractString; <keywords>)\n\nOpen and read a FITS file, returning a vector of header-data units (HDUs). The first HDU is the primary HDU and the others are extensions (Sect. 3.1 of the standard), and a file whose HDUs are not in this order is read with a warning. The special records after the last HDU (Sect. 3.5) are not read, with a warning.\n\nThe default data structure for Random and Bintable HDUs is a named tuple of arrays.\n\nThe data of the HDUs of a file are not read until they are accessed (hdu.data), so that a file is scanned quickly, e.g., to info its HDUs, and size(hdu) is the size of the data from the header. They are read from the file each time they are accessed, so the file must not have been modified since it was read, which is an ArgumentError, and an HDU that is written is written with the data as they are in the file (see Base.write(io::IO, hdu::HDU)). The keyword access chooses how the data of a file are accessed: :lazy is this default, :memory reads all of the data at once, and :mapped maps the data of the primary HDU, the IMAGE extensions, the conforming HDUs, and random groups, so that hdu.data is a read-only array that reads only the values that are indexed (see mapdata and Base.read(io::IO, ::Type{HDU})).\n\nKeywords\n\nchecksum::Bool=false: verify the DATASUM and CHECKSUM keywords of each HDU, with a warning if a sum is not the sum of the data or of the HDU (Sect. 4.4.2.7 of the standard)\nrecord::Bool=false: structure the data as a list of records\nscale::Bool=true: apply the scale and zero keywords to the data; the data of a BSCALE of 1 and a BZERO of 0 are the stored values, e.g., the integers of an integer image, and the scaled values of 32- and 64-bit integers are Float64; an image of the offsets of Table 11 of the standard (BSCALE of 1 and BZERO of 2^15, 2^31, or 2^63 for 16-, 32-, or 64-bit integers, or -128 for bytes) is an array of UInt16, UInt32, UInt64, or Int8, and so is the array of random groups, and a parameter that has the PSCALn of 1 and the PZEROn of the offsets; floating-point data with a scale and a zero that are not 1 and 0 are a FITSFiles.ScaledArray, which has them\nstrict::Bool=true: numbers with a lower case exponent letter (d or e) are invalid cards, unless false\nunits::Union{Bool, Module}=true: the units in the comment of a number are the units of the value (true is Unitful, or the module DynamicQuantities when it is loaded), unless false\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.fits"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.info-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:HDU}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"info(hdus::AbstractVector{<:HDU})\n\nBriefly describe the list of header-data units (HDUs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.info"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.specialrecords-Tuple{IO, Integer}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"specialrecords(io, n)\n\nReturn true, with a warning, if the bytes of io that follow the nth HDU are special records (Sect. 3.5 of the standard), and move to the end of io. Special records are the blocks after the last HDU, which do not meet the requirements of a conforming extension, so that the first eight bytes of them are not XTENSION. The first eight bytes of the header of an HDU after the first are XTENSION, and SIMPLE is read as an HDU that is in the wrong place (see verifyposition). The special records are not read, and are not written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.specialrecords"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyname-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyname(hdus)\n\nWarn if the last HDU of the vector hdus has the same type, EXTNAME, EXTVER, and EXTLEVEL as an HDU before it. It is recommended that the combination of these keywords is unique in each HDU of a FITS file that has them (Sect. 4.4.2.6 of the standard). The type of an HDU is the value of its XTENSION, and the primary array is equivalent to an IMAGE extension, but random groups are not: their type is GROUPS. An absent EXTVER or EXTLEVEL is 1, and the HDUs that have none of the three keywords are not compared. The HDUs are read and written as they are. Return the HDUs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyname"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyposition-Tuple{HDU, Integer}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyposition(hdu, j)\n\nWarn if the jth HDU of a FITS file is not what the standard requires of its position (Sect. 3.1): the first HDU is the primary HDU, whose header begins with SIMPLE, and the HDUs after it are conforming extensions, whose headers begin with XTENSION, and so do not have SIMPLE (Sect. 3.4.3). A file that has HDUs that are not in this order is read and written as it is. Return the HDU. The EXTEND keyword of the primary header is advisory (Sect. 4.4.2.1), so it is not verified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyposition"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.HDU","page":"FITSFiles","text":"HDU{T}(cards, data)\nHDU([data, [cards]]; <keywords>)\nHDU(cards; <keywords>)\nHDU(type, missing, [cards]; <keywords>)\nHDU(type, data, [cards]; <keywords>)\n\nCreate a header-data unit described by data and cards of type AbstractHDU.\n\nThe HDU function tries to determine the HDU type based on the data and cards. If only data is provided, then an array is interpreted as a Primary HDU and a Tuple or NamedTuple as a Bintable. If mandatory cards are provided, then they are used to determine the HDU type. Otherwise, the HDU type must be specified.\n\nHDU types are: Primary, Random, Image, Table, Bintable, and Conform.\n\nArguments\n\ndata::U=missing: the binary or ASCII data, where U <: Union{AbstractArray,  Tuple, NamedTuple, Missing}\ncards::U=missing: the list of cards, where U <: Union{Card, Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}, Missing}\n\nKeywords\n\nrecord::Bool=false: structure the data as a list of records\nscale::Bool=true: apply the scale and zero keywords to the data; floating-point data that have a scale and a zero that are not 1 and 0 are a FITSFiles.ScaledArray, which has them, and is written as the stored values\n\nThe following keywords format the cards that are created, and are those of Card, which describes them.\n\nappend::Bool=false: append an ampersand to a string that is continued by a CONTINUE card\nfixed::Bool=true: create fixed format cards\nslash::Integer=32: index of the comment separator (/)\nlpad::Integer=1: number of spaces before the comment separator\nrpad::Integer=1: number of spaces after the comment separator\nupad::Integer=1: number of spaces after the units\ntruncate::Bool=true: truncate a comment that is longer than the space at the end of the card\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.HDU"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.findall-Tuple{AbstractString, Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"findall(key, cards)\n\nReturn the indexes of all the cards with the keyword key, in the order of the header. The indexes are empty if there is no such card.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.findall"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.get-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, Union{Tuple, AbstractArray}, Union{Tuple, AbstractArray}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"get(cards, key, default)\nget(cards, keys, defaults)\n\nReturn the value of the first card with the keyword key, or default if there is no such card. With a list of keys, return the tuple of the values of the first cards with the keywords, in the order of the keys, where the default of a keyword that is not found is the default at the same position. The types of the values are the types of the cards, and not the types of the defaults. The value of a continued string is the joined string. See getall for the values of all the cards with a keyword.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.get"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.read-Tuple{IO, Type{HDU}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.read(io::IO, ::Type{HDU}; <keywords>)\n\nRead the next header-data unit (HDU) from io and return it. The data of an HDU that is read from a file are by default a lazy array, which is read when the data are accessed (access = :lazy), and the data of an HDU that is read from another stream are read at once. The fill of the last data block is zeros, or ASCII spaces for an ASCII table, and a warning says if it is not (see verifyfill).\n\nKeywords\n\naccess::Symbol=:lazy: how the data of an HDU that is read from a file are accessed. :lazy is the default: the file is scanned without reading the data, which are read when hdu.data is accessed, as an ordinary array that can be changed. :memory reads the data at once. :mapped maps the data of the primary HDU, an IMAGE extension, a conforming HDU, and random groups that are not records in memory (see mapdata), so that hdu.data is a read-only array (for random groups, of arrays) that reads only the values that are indexed from the file, and the data of the other types of HDU are lazy. The file must not be modified while its data are lazy or mapped. A stream that is not a file is read at once, and :mapped is an error for it.\ntype=nothing: the HDU type, e.g., Image, which is otherwise determined by the mandatory keywords of the header\nstrict::Bool=true: numbers with a lower case exponent letter (d or e) are invalid cards, unless false\nunits::Union{Bool, Module}=true: the units in the comment of a number are the units of the value (true is Unitful, or the module DynamicQuantities when it is loaded), unless false\nrecord::Bool=false: structure the data as a list of records\nscale::Bool=true: apply the scale and zero keywords to the data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.read"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.arrangecards!-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, Any}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"arrangecards!(cards, required)\n\nReturn the cards with the required cards first, in the order of required, and the remaining cards of cards after them. required is a list of key => value pairs. A required card that is in cards is moved to its place, with its comment and layout, and the others are made from the values. The END card is removed, since it is implied and is written by write.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.arrangecards!"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.extname-Tuple{HDU}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"extname(hdu)\nextver(hdu)\nextlevel(hdu)\n\nReturn the value of the EXTNAME, EXTVER, or EXTLEVEL keyword of the header-data unit hdu (Sect. 4.4.2.6 of the standard), which distinguish the extensions of a file. The name is an empty string if the keyword is absent, and the version and the level are 1, as the standard says, if the keyword is absent. The trailing spaces of the name, which are not significant, are removed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.extname"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.getall-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"getall(cards, key)\ngetall(cards, keys)\n\nReturn the values of all the cards with the keyword key, in the order of the header, e.g., the values of the COMMENT or HISTORY cards, which are the vector of the values, or an empty vector if there is no such card. With a list of keys, return the tuple of the vectors of the keys. The value of a continued string is the joined string, and the CONTINUE cards of the string are not values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.getall"},{"category":"function","location":"#FITSFiles.info","page":"FITSFiles","text":"info(hdu::HDU)\n\nBriefly describe the header-data unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.info"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.joinvalue-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, Integer}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"joinvalue(cards, j)\n\nReturn the value of the card at index j. The value of a continued string is the value of the card joined with the CONTINUE cards that follow it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.joinvalue"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.mapdata-Tuple{HDU}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"mapdata(hdu::HDU)\n\nThe data array of an HDU that is read from a file, memory-mapped, so that the values of the array are read from the file when they are indexed, and the file is not read at once. It is an AbstractArray of the values that hdu.data is, of the same type and with the same scale, and has the same size, e.g., a FITSFiles.ScaledArray of the values of floating-point stored values that have a BSCALE and a BZERO. It is for the primary HDU, the IMAGE extension, and the conforming HDU, i.e., for the data of an array, and for random groups, whose data are a NamedTuple of the mapped parameters and array (for repeated names a matrix of the parameters), which are read by the group and not by the file, and an error for the data of another type of HDU, or of random groups that are records. The data of the HDUs of a file are mapped when it is read by the keyword access = :mapped of fits and read(io, HDU), so that hdu.data is the mapped array.\n\nSlices, e.g., array[1:100, 5, :], and collect(array) read the values at once, but each value of an array that is read by value is read with a function call. The array reads the file as it is when it is indexed, so it is not valid if the file is truncated or changed. It is an ArgumentError if the file has changed since the HDU was read, or does not have the data of the header. An HDU whose data are read, e.g., from a buffer, or is made, has its data returned as they are, and an HDU that has no data returns missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.mapdata"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.readbytes-Tuple{IO, Integer}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"readbytes(io, n)\n\nRead the n bytes of the data of an HDU. An error says that the file is shorter than the header says if it has fewer bytes, instead of the data failing to match the shape of the array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.readbytes"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.setvalue!-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractString, Any}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"setvalue!(cards, key, value; <keywords>)\n\nReplace the value of the first card with the keyword key. A string that does not fit on a card is split into the cards of a long string, and the CONTINUE cards of a string that is replaced are removed. The keywords, i.e., fixed, append, slash, lpad, rpad, upad, truncate, and splitat, are those of setvalue!(card, value), where the default of a layout keyword is the layout of the card. The cards are always replaced, which is the way to split the value again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.setvalue!"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.typeofhdu-Tuple{U} where U<:Union{Missing, Tuple, AbstractArray, NamedTuple}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"typeofhdu(data)\ntypeofhdu(dict)\ntypeofhdu(data, dict)\n\nDetermine the HDU type based on the data structure, the mandatory keywords, or both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.typeofhdu"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.units-Tuple{Card}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"units(card)\nunits(cards, key)\n\nReturn the units of a card, or of the first card of a list of cards with the keyword, which are nothing if the value has no units. The units are not part of the value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.units"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verify!-Tuple{Type{<:FITSFiles.AbstractHDU}, Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractDict, AbstractDict}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verify!(type, cards, mankeys, reskeys)\n\nVerify the values of the mandatory and reserved keywords of a header, mankeys and reskeys, before the data format is created from them. Return the cards. A verify! method of an HDU type corrects the cards and the mandatory keywords, mankeys, when it is possible, and otherwise raises an error that names the keyword and its value. The generic method raises an error if the value of a keyword that must be an integer is not (see verifyinteger), and warns if the XTENSION value is not padded.\n\nThe verify! methods that also have a data format argument are for the HDU types that create the format from data and then correct the header to match the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verify!"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyaxes-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyaxes(mankeys)\n\nVerify the NAXISn keywords of an HDU, from NAXIS1 to NAXIS, in the dictionary mankeys of the mandatory keywords. It is an error, since the size of the data cannot be determined, if NAXISn is missing for an n from 1 to NAXIS, or if it is negative. It is a warning, since the extra keywords are not used, if NAXISn is present for an n greater than NAXIS. A header with no NAXIS is not verified. Random groups, whose NAXIS1 is 0, have their own method (Sects. 4.4.1.1, 7.1.1, 7.2.1, and 7.3.1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyaxes"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifycounts!-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractDict, FITSFiles.DataFormat}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifycounts!(cards, mankeys, format)\n\nCorrect PCOUNT and GCOUNT of cards to match the data format format, and return the cards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifycounts!"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifydate-Tuple{AbstractDict, Regex}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifydate(keys, pattern)\n\nWarn if the value of a keyword of the dictionary keys that matches pattern is not a date of the form YYYY-MM-DD or YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss[.sss...], or DD/MM/YY for a date before 2000 (Sect. 4.4.2.1 of the standard). The value is read and written as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifydate"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyfill-Tuple{IO, Type{<:FITSFiles.AbstractHDU}, Integer, Integer}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyfill(io, type, pos, nbytes)\n\nWarn if the fill of the last data block after the nbytes bytes of the data of an HDU of the type type, which begin at the position pos of io, is not what the standard says: all bits zero, or ASCII spaces for an ASCII table (Sects. 3.3.2, 6.2, 7.2.3, and 7.3.3). The data are read as they are, but the fill is not kept, and is written as it should be. A last block that is not filled, which is a file that ends after the data, is not verified. The position of io is not kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyfill"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyfixed-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyfixed(cards, required)\n\nWarn if the value of a card of the keywords required is not in fixed format, and return the cards unchanged (see verifyprimary).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyfixed"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyfloat-Tuple{AbstractDict, Regex}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyfloat(keys, pattern)\n\nWarn if the value of a keyword of the dictionary keys that matches pattern is an integer, when the standard requires a floating-point number, e.g., DATAMIN = 0. A value that is not a number is not checked (see verifynumber). The value is read as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyfloat"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyinteger-Tuple{AbstractDict, Regex}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyinteger(keys, pattern)\n\nRaise an ArgumentError that names the keyword and its value if the value of a keyword of the dictionary keys that matches the regular expression pattern is not an integer, e.g., NAXIS1 = 2.0 or PCOUNT = 'two'. keys is the dictionary of the mandatory keywords, mankeys, or of the reserved keywords, reskeys, of a header (see MANDATORYKEYS and RESERVEDKEYS), and a verify! method calls verifyinteger once for each. A floating-point value, even of a whole number, is not an integer, and the size of the data cannot be determined from it, so the HDU cannot be read. The values of other keywords are not checked.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyinteger"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifylogical-Tuple{AbstractDict, Regex}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifylogical(keys, pattern)\n\nWarn if the value of a keyword of the dictionary keys that matches pattern is not a logical, T or F. The value is read and written as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifylogical"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifynumber-Tuple{AbstractDict, Regex}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifynumber(keys, pattern)\n\nRaise an ArgumentError that names the keyword and its value if the value of a keyword of the dictionary keys that matches pattern is not a real number, e.g., BSCALE = 'two'. A value of an integer type is a number, since a keyword of a floating-point number can be written without a decimal point. The array cannot be scaled by a value that is not a number, so the HDU cannot be read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifynumber"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyorder-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyorder(cards, required)\n\nWarn at the first of the first cards that is not the keyword in the same place of required, and return the cards unchanged (see verifyprimary). The keywords section and names are the section of the standard and the names of the keywords that the warning gives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyorder"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyshape!-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractDict, FITSFiles.DataFormat}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyshape!(cards, mankeys, format)\n\nCorrect BITPIX and the NAXIS keywords of cards to match the data format format, and return the cards. The data are described by NAXIS1 and the keywords after it. A keyword that the cards do not have, e.g., NAXIS when they have NAXIS1, is added. Random groups, whose NAXIS1 is 0, have their own method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyshape!"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifystring-Tuple{AbstractDict, Regex}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifystring(keys, pattern)\n\nWarn if the value of a keyword of the dictionary keys that matches pattern is not a character string. The value is read and written as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifystring"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyunit-Tuple{AbstractDict, Regex}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyunit(keys, pattern)\n\nWarn if the value of a keyword of the dictionary keys that matches pattern is a character string that is not a units string (Sect. 4.3 of the standard). An empty string is not checked. The value is read and written as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyunit"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyvalue-Tuple{AbstractDict, AbstractString, Any, AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyvalue(keys, key, allowed, requirement)\n\nRaise an ArgumentError that names the keyword and its value if the keyword key is in the dictionary keys, of the mandatory keywords, and its value is not in allowed, e.g., a range or a tuple of values. requirement describes the allowed values for the message, e.g., \"the integer 0\". A keyword that is not in keys is not verified, since the header of an HDU that is created without cards has no keywords. The value is an integer, which verifyinteger verifies first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyvalue"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyxtension!-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyxtension!(cards)\n\nWarn if the value of the XTENSION card is not padded to 8 characters, as the standard requires, and return the cards unchanged. A file may have the value written without the padding, e.g., 'TABLE', which is read without error, since the trailing spaces of a string are not significant, and the file is written as it was read, so the card is not changed. The width of the value is the width of the string on the card, and not the value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyxtension!"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.leastfloat-Tuple{Union{Nothing, Real}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"leastfloat(real::Union{Real, Nothing})\n\nConvert an integer to the smallest float type of Float32, Float64, and BigFloat that holds the integer, i.e., a Float32 for a 32-bit integer, a Float64 for a 64-bit integer, and otherwise a BigFloat. A value that is not an integer is returned as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.leastfloat"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.scaled-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"scaled(data, scale, zero)\n\nReturn zero + scale*data. The type of the result is the promotion of the types of data, scale and zero (a Float32 for 32-bit integer data and a Float32 scale, say), but the operands are converted to at least a Float64 before the multiplication and the addition, which muladd fuses into a single rounding where the hardware allows it. A Float32 product would otherwise round the stored integers to 24 bits (e.g., a UInt32 stored as Int32 with TZERO 2147483648 and a Float32 TSCAL of 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.scaled"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.setmissing-Tuple{Any, Nothing}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"setmissing(data, miss)\n\nReturn data with missing in place of the raw values equal to miss, the value of the BLANK or TNULLn keyword (Sects. 4.4.2.5 and 7.3.2 of the standard). The comparison is made before any scaling. The result has the element type Union{T, Missing}, whether or not a value is equal to miss, and data itself is returned when miss is nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.setmissing"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.unsetmissing-Tuple{Any, Any, Type}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"unsetmissing(data, miss, type)\n\nReturn data with miss in place of missing, as raw values of type: the inverse of setmissing. Data of a floating-point type has no BLANK or TNULLn keyword, and the undefined value is NaN (Sect. 4.4.2.5). It is an ArgumentError if data has a missing value but no miss is given for an integer type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.unsetmissing"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.DataFormat","page":"FITSFiles","text":"DataFormat(type, leng, shape, param, group, heap)\n\nThe generic data format descriptor for HDUs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.DataFormat"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.LazyArray","page":"FITSFiles","text":"LazyArray(filnam, mtime, begpos, format, fields, keywds)\n\nThe lazy array file descriptor of the data of an HDU, which is read from the file when the data is accessed. It is the place of the data of an HDU that a file is scanned for, so that the HDUs of the file are read without reading their data, which filnam is the absolute path of, and mtime is the modification time of when the HDU was read. The data are read from the file, or are copied as they are when the HDU is written, only if the file has not changed (see verifylazy).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.LazyArray"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifylazy-Tuple{FITSFiles.LazyArray}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifylazy(data::LazyArray)\n\nReturn the lazy array data of the data of an HDU, or raise an ArgumentError if the file that the data are read from does not exist, or has been modified since the HDU was read, since the position and the size of the data that the header says are not those of the file then.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifylazy"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.ScaledArray","page":"FITSFiles","text":"ScaledArray(values, scale, zero)\n\nThe physical values of floating-point data that were read with a BSCALE and a BZERO that are not 1 and 0 (Eq. 3 of the standard), or with a PSCALn and a PZEROn (Eq. 5), together with that scale and zero, which have the type of the stored values. The stored values and the physical values of floating-point data have the same type, so the type does not say which of them an array is. The array that is a ScaledArray is the physical values, and is written as the stored values (value - zero)/scale, and the header has the BSCALE and BZERO (or PSCALn and PZEROn) of the array. The array that is made from a ScaledArray by an operation of its values, e.g., a broadcast or a slice, is a plain array, and is the stored values, which are written as they are, and copy is a ScaledArray.\n\nThe scale and the zero are numbers, or vectors with a number for each column of a matrix of the parameters of one name of random groups (Sect. 6.1.2). The values are the values of parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.ScaledArray"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.MappedArray","page":"FITSFiles","text":"MappedArray(values, size, decode; value, probe, source)\n\nThe data array of an HDU that is read in place from its file, which is memory-mapped, so that only the values that are indexed are read from the file. The values are the values of the array of the file, in the byte order of the file (big-endian) and the order of the array (the first axis varies fastest), whose shape is size, e.g., a memory map of the data of the HDU, and decode is the function of an array of the values that are in the byte order of the machine to the values of the HDU, which are scaled and have missing for the undefined values, as the array that is read is. The keyword value is the function of one value, which is decode of an array of one value if it is not given, and the type of the values is that of the values of the keyword probe, a vector of values of the file. The source is the lazy array of the HDU that the array is read from, which the data are copied from when the HDU is written. The array is read from the file as the file is when it is indexed, so it is not valid if the file is truncated or changed. It is read-only.\n\nA slice of the array, e.g., array[1:100, 5, :], and collect(array) read the values at once, and a value that is indexed is read with the function value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.MappedArray"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Primary","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Primary header-data unit (HDU)\n\nA Primary HDU consists of a vector of cards and optionally a multidimensional array (<=999 dimensions). The Primary HDU is the first HDU in a vector of HDUs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Primary"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.PrimaryField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"PrimaryField(type, zero, scale, miss, dmin, dmax)\n\nPrimary array element descriptor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.PrimaryField"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.size-Tuple{HDU{Primary}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.size(hdu::HDU{Primary})\n\nThe dimensions of the data array of a primary HDU, which are the NAXISn of its header, so that the size of an HDU whose data are not read yet (see fits) is known without reading them. A primary HDU that has no data has no dimensions, ().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.size"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyprimary-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractDict}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyprimary(cards, mankeys)\n\nWarn if the header of a primary HDU does not begin with SIMPLE, BITPIX, NAXIS, and the NAXISn keywords, in that order and with no other keyword between them (Sect. 4.4.1.1 of the standard), or if the values of these keywords are not in fixed format (Sect. 4.4.1), and return the cards unchanged. The file is read and written as it is, so the cards are not reordered or reformatted. The NAXIS value of mankeys must be valid, as verifyarray checks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyprimary"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.ConformField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"ConformField()\n\nConforming array element descriptor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.ConformField"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.size-Tuple{HDU{Conform}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.size(hdu::HDU{Conform})\n\nThe dimensions of the data array of a conforming HDU, which are the NAXISn of its header, so that the size of an HDU whose data are not read yet (see fits) is known without reading them. An HDU that has no data has no dimensions, (). The data of an HDU that has a heap or groups (PCOUNT and GCOUNT) are a vector, which does not have the size of the NAXISn.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.size"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyconform-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractDict}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyconform(cards, mankeys)\n\nWarn if the header of the primary HDU of a Conform HDU does not begin with SIMPLE, BITPIX, NAXIS, and the NAXISn keywords, in that order and with no other keyword between them (Sect. 4.4.1.1 of the standard), or if the values of these keywords are not in fixed format (Sect. 4.4.1), and return the cards unchanged. The file is read and written as it is, so the cards are not reordered or reformatted. The NAXIS value of mankeys must be valid, as verifyarray checks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyconform"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Random","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Random header-data unit (HDU)\n\nA Random HDU consists of a vector of cards and optionally a vector of records containing a list of parameters followed by a multidimensional array. The Random HDU is the first HDU in a vector of HDUs.\n\nThe data are a named tuple of arrays, or, with record = true, a vector of named tuples, one for each group. The parameters are named by their PTYPEn keywords (or param1, param2, ... if there are none) and the array of a group is named data, so a parameter whose PTYPEn is data is named param<n> (see the warning). The groups are along the first axis of the array. Parameters that have the same name are the columns of one array, whose sum along its columns is the value of the parameter (Sect. 6.1.2 of the standard), so that the parameters are written as they were read, and sum(data.DATE; dims = 2) (sum(record.DATE) in a record) is the value of the parameter DATE. The data are written by position, so the names of the data do not have to be the names of the header.\n\nThe parameters and the array have the same type, which is the type of BITPIX (Sect. 6.3). The data are read with PSCALn and PZEROn applied to the parameters and BSCALE and BZERO to the array, unless scale = false is given, and the values that are not of the type of BITPIX are written as the stored values (see Base.write(io::IO, hdu::HDU)). A parameter with the PSCALn of 1 and the PZEROn of the offsets of Table 11 (Sect. 5.2.5, which Sect. 6.3 gives to the parameters), and the array with the BSCALE of 1 and the BZERO of the offsets, are the unsigned integers or the signed bytes: UInt16, UInt32, UInt64, or Int8, for the values of 16-, 32-, or 64-bit integers, or bytes. Data of those types have the PZEROn and BZERO keywords of the offsets when the HDU is made.\n\nA header with an NAXIS of 1 has no axes in the array of a group, and so one value in each group (Eq. 6 of the standard), which is a vector of the groups in the data, and a number in a record. Data of a vector are a table by default, so make them with HDU(Random, data).\n\nThe elements of the array of integers that are the stored value of BLANK are missing (Sect. 4.4.2.5), with and without scale = false, and are written as BLANK. The parameters do not have undefined values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Random"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.RandomField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"RandomField(name, index, type, slice, leng, shape, zero, scale, miss)\n\nRandom array element descriptor. miss is the value of BLANK, the stored value of an undefined element, for the array of a group (Sect. 4.4.2.5), and is nothing for a parameter and for an array without a BLANK.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.RandomField"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.ScaledRecords","page":"FITSFiles","text":"ScaledRecords(records, scales, zeros)\n\nThe records of random groups (record = true) whose values are the physical values of floating-point stored values (see ScaledArray): the parameters and the array of each record, which have the type of the stored values, and are written as the stored values. The vectors scales and zeros have the PSCALn and PZEROn of the parameters, and the BSCALE and BZERO of the array, in order. The records are the values of parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.ScaledRecords"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.size-Tuple{HDU{Random}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.size(hdu::HDU{Random})\n\nThe dimensions of the array of the groups of an HDU of random groups, which are the GCOUNT groups of the first dimension and the NAXISn of the array of a group (NAXIS2 to NAXIS), so that the size of an HDU whose data are not read yet (see fits) is known without reading them. It is the size of the data array of the data. A group that has no axes, which a header with a NAXIS of 1 has, has one value, and the size is the number of groups, (GCOUNT,).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.size"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Image","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Image header-data unit (HDU)\n\nAn Image HDU consists of a vector of cards and optionally a multidimensional array (<=999 dimensions).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Image"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.ImageField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"ImageField(type, zero, scale, miss, dmin, dmax)\n\nImage array element descriptor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.ImageField"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.size-Tuple{HDU{Image}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.size(hdu::HDU{Image})\n\nThe dimensions of the data array of an IMAGE extension, which are the NAXISn of its header, so that the size of an HDU whose data are not read yet (see fits) is known without reading them. An IMAGE extension that has no data has no dimensions, ().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.size"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.ScaledRows","page":"FITSFiles","text":"ScaledRows(records, scales)\n\nThe records of an ASCII table (record = true) that has fields of floating-point values, or of exact integers, that are scaled by a TSCALn and a TZEROn. The values of those fields are the physical values (see ScaledArray), which the dictionary scales has the scale and the zero of, by the name of the field, and are written as the stored values. The records are the values of parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.ScaledRows"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Table","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Table header-data unit (HDU)\n\nA Table HDU consists of a vector of cards and optionally a 2-dimensional array of ASCII data, which is a named tuple of the columns of the fields, or a vector of records, a named tuple for each row (record = true), or a vector of the strings of the rows.\n\nThe fields of the table that is made from data have the formats TFORMn and the first columns TBCOLn of the cards that are given, and otherwise the format of the values of the field and the column that follows the one space after the previous field. The keywords formats and columns of HDU(Table, data, cards; formats, columns) are vectors that have a format string (Table 15 of the standard, e.g., \"F8.3\") and a first column (the first column of a row is 1) for each field, and override the cards.\n\nThe values of a field of type A are strings, of type I are Int64, and of types F, E, and D are Float64, with missing for an undefined value, which is the TNULLn string of the field (Sect. 7.2.2 of the standard). A blank field is the value 0, as the standard says (Sect. 7.2.5). The values of a field that has a TSCALn and a TZEROn that are not 1 and 0 are the physical values, which are Float64 (Eq. 7), unless scale = false. The values of an integer field whose TSCALn is 1 and TZEROn is an integer are exact integers (Int64, or BigInt if they do not fit), which are a ScaledArray. The integers of an integer field that do not fit an Int64 are a column of BigInt, since the standard has no limit (Sect. 7.2.5). The real numbers of the data are written with the digits that they need if the field has room for them, which is more than the d of the format if they need it (the formats of Table 15 are input formats, Sect. 7.2.1), so that what is read is written again.\n\nThe data are matched to the fields by name: a named tuple of columns and the records that are named tuples have a value for each field of its TTYPEn, in any order. The fields without a TTYPEn are named column1, column2, and so on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Table"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.TableField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"TableField(name, type, slice, unit, form, disp, zero, scale, null,\n    dmin, dmax, lmin, lmax)\n\nTable array element descriptor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.TableField"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.size-Tuple{HDU{Table}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.size(hdu::HDU{Table})\n\nThe size of the two-dimensional array of characters of an ASCII table extension, which is the number of characters in a row and the number of rows, the NAXIS1 and the NAXIS2 of its header (Sect. 7.2.3 of the standard), so that the size of an HDU whose data are not read yet (see fits) is known without reading them. The number of rows is also the number of values of each column of the data, and TFIELDS is the number of columns. A header that does not have them has none, (0, 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.size"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyfields!-Tuple{Type{Table}, Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractDict}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyfields!(Table, cards, mankeys; created = false)\n\nVerify the keywords that describe the fields of an ASCII table extension, and return the cards unchanged (Sect. 7.2.1 of the standard). It is an error, since the fields cannot be read, if TFIELDS is negative, if TFORMn is missing or is not one of the formats of Table 15 (Aw, Iw, Fw.d, Ew.d, or Dw.d, in upper case) for an n from 1 to TFIELDS, if TBCOLn is missing or less than 1, since the first column of a row is numbered 1, or if a field is not within the NAXIS1 characters of a row. A header that is being created (created) does not need TFORMn and TBCOLn, which are made from the data. It is a warning, since the extra keywords are not used, if TFIELDS is more than 999, the maximum, or if TFORMn or TBCOLn is present for an n greater than TFIELDS. A header with no TFIELDS is not verified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyfields!"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.BinaryField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"BinaryField(name, pntr, type, slice, leng, supp, unit, disp, dims,\n    zero, scale, null, dmin, dmax, lmin, lmax)\n\nBinary array element descriptor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.BinaryField"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Bintable","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Bintable header-data unit (HDU)\n\nA Bintable HDU consists of a vector of cards and optionally a 2-dimensional array of binary data structured as a vector of records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Bintable"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.ScaledTable","page":"FITSFiles","text":"ScaledTable(records, scales)\n\nThe records of a binary table (record = true) that has fields of floating-point or complex stored values that are scaled by a TSCALn and a TZEROn. The values of those fields are the physical values (see ScaledArray), which the dictionary scales has the scale and the zero of, by the name of the field, and are written as the stored values. The records are the values of parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.ScaledTable"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.size-Tuple{HDU{Bintable}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.size(hdu::HDU{Bintable})\n\nThe size of the two-dimensional byte array of the main table of a binary table extension, which is the number of bytes in a row and the number of rows, the NAXIS1 and the NAXIS2 of its header (Sect. 7.3.3 of the standard), so that the size of an HDU whose data are not read yet (see fits) is known without reading them. The number of rows is also the number of values of each column of the data, and TFIELDS is the number of columns. A header that does not have them has none, (0, 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.size"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.parsedims-Tuple{Union{Nothing, AbstractString}, Integer}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"parsedims(value, j)\n\nReturn the dimensions of the TDIMj keyword as a tuple of integers, or nothing if there is no keyword. The value is a character string of the form (l,m,n...) (Sect. 7.3.2 of the standard), where l, m, n, ... are the dimensions, and a single dimension, (l), is the tuple (l,). A value that is not of this form is not read: it gives a warning and nothing, and the keyword stays in the header unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.parsedims"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyfields!-Tuple{Type{Bintable}, Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractDict}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyfields!(Bintable, cards, mankeys)\n\nVerify the keywords that describe the fields of a binary table extension, and return the cards unchanged (Sect. 7.3.1 of the standard). It is an error, since the fields cannot be read, if TFIELDS is negative, or if TFORMn is missing for an n from 1 to TFIELDS. It is a warning, since the extra keywords are not used, if TFIELDS is more than 999, the maximum, or if TFORMn is present for an n greater than TFIELDS. A header with no TFIELDS is not verified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyfields!"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.verifyheap!-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractDict}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"verifyheap!(cards, mankeys)\n\nWarn if THEAP of a binary table is outside of the range that the standard allows (Sect. 7.3.2), and return the cards unchanged. The heap starts THEAP bytes after the start of the main data table, so THEAP cannot be less than NAXIS1*NAXIS2 or more than that plus PCOUNT, the bytes of the gap and the heap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.verifyheap!"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Card","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Card(type, key, value, [units,] comment, format)\nCard([key, [value, [comment]]]; <keywords>)\nCard(\"HIERARCH\", tokens, value, comment; <keywords>)\n\nCreate a card type, where type is Comment, Continue, End, Hierarch, History, Invalid, or Value{T}.\n\nArguments\n\nkey::AbstractString=\"\": keyword string\nvalue::U=missing: keyword value, where U is Missing, Bool, Integer, AbstractFloat, a Complex number of integers or floats, or AbstractString, which is the type T of the card Value{T}. Another type of value is an ArgumentError. A Quantity of a number may be given in place of the number, but a Quantity is not a type of value: the value of the card is the number, and the units of the Quantity are the units of the card. The units of a number without a Quantity are the unit string in square brackets at the beginning of the comment, e.g., [km/s] velocity, as they are for an image, and the unit string is removed from the comment. The comment of a string, a logical, a mandatory keyword, text that is not a unit string, or a scale factor, is not units.\ntokens::AbstractString: the tokens of a HIERARCH keyword, which are separated by spaces\ncomment::AbstractString=\"\": comment string\n\nKeywords\n\nappend::Bool=false: append an ampersand to the string value, which marks that the string is continued by a CONTINUE card. A string that is longer than the card (>68 characters) is always split into a card and CONTINUE cards, which are returned as a vector.\nfixed::Bool=true: use fixed format. A fixed-format number ends at byte 30, so a Float64 that has more than 20 characters is written with fewer digits of the mantissa, which are truncated, e.g., 1.2345678901234567e-100 is written as 1.2345678901234D-100. Use fixed=false to keep all of the digits.\nslash::Integer=32: index of the comment separator (/). A value that passes the separator moves the separator to the right. A number in fixed format ends at the index 30, so the separator is at the index 32 or later. A comment, and the units, must end at or before the end of the card. If there is no space for the comment after the separator, the comment is dropped, or is continued at the index 32 on the CONTINUE cards of a string if truncate is false.\nlpad::Integer=1: number of spaces before comment separator\nrpad::Integer=1: number of spaces after comment separator\nupad::Integer=1: number of spaces after the units string\ntruncate::Bool=true: truncate a comment that is longer than the space at the end of the card, and the comment of the card is the truncated comment. If false, the comment of a string is continued on CONTINUE cards of the string, and a comment of another value that does not fit, and units that do not fit, are an ArgumentError. A comment is dropped if the comment separator is at the end of the card.\nwarn::Bool=true: warn when a keyword longer than 8 characters creates a HIERARCH card\nsplitat::Union{Char, Nothing}=' ': split a long string and its comment into the cards after the last character that fits, instead of splitting at any character if nothing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Card"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.CardFormat","page":"FITSFiles","text":"CardFormat(fixd, vbeg, vend, ampr, slsh, ubeg, uend, cbeg, cend)\n\nCreate a card format descriptor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.CardFormat"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.parsecomplex-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"parsecomplex(real::AbstractString, imag::AbstractString)\n\nDetermine the type of a complex number as the smallest type that holds both parts. The parts are integers if both are integers, and otherwise floats, where an integer is a float of a type that holds the integer exactly, i.e., a Float32, a Float64, or a BigFloat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.parsecomplex"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.parsenumber-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"parsenumber(real::AbstractString)\n\nDetermine the type of a number as a Float32, Float64, Int64, Int128, or BigInt. A floating-point number is a Float32 only if the Float32 is exactly the Float64 of its text, so that no precision is lost, e.g., 0.5 is a Float32, but 0.1 and 266.4 are a Float64. A number with a D exponent is a Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.parsenumber"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.printfmt-Tuple{AbstractString, Number}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"printfmt(vfmt, value)\n\nReturn the text of a number with the printf format vfmt. The conversion e is for a number with a D exponent, which is written as a D. The format may end with |-, which writes the exponent with a sign only if it is negative and without leading zeros, e.g., E6.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.printfmt"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.joincards-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"joincards(cards)\n\nJoin CONTINUE cards to initial long string card to create a long value and comment card. Cards after the end of the continued string are ignored. The comments of the cards are joined with a space, unless a comment ends with a space, since the spaces at the end of the comment of an image are not read, e.g., a comment that is split at any character has a space at the split.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.joincards"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.setvalue!-Tuple{Card, Any}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"setvalue!(card, value; <keywords>)\n\nReturn the card with the value value, and the units, format, and comment of the card. A number without units has the units of the card, or the units at the beginning of the comment of the card if the card has no units, as for the constructor of a card. A string that fits on the card, i.e., that has at most 68 characters, is not split, and moves the comment to the right if the string passes the comment separator. A string that does not fit on the card is split into the cards of a long string, the first card and the CONTINUE cards, which are returned as a vector, at the comment separator, or at the end of the card if there is no comment. The keywords are those of the constructor of a card, where the default of a layout keyword is the layout of the card.\n\nKeywords\n\nfixed::Bool: use fixed format. The default is the format of the card.\nappend::Bool=false: append an ampersand to the string value, which marks that the string is continued by a CONTINUE card.\nslash::Integer: index of the comment separator (/). The default is the index of the separator of the card, and the index 32 or more for the separator of the cards of a long string.\nlpad::Integer: number of spaces before the comment separator. The default is 0 if the separator follows the value of the card directly, and otherwise 1.\nrpad::Integer: number of spaces after the comment separator. The default is the number of spaces of the card, or 1 if the card has no comment.\nupad::Integer: number of spaces after the units string. The default is the number of spaces of the card, or 1 if the card has no units.\ntruncate::Bool=true: truncate a comment that is longer than the space at the end of the card, and otherwise continue the comment on the CONTINUE cards of a string, or raise an ArgumentError for another value.\nsplitat::Union{Char, Nothing}=' ': split a long string and its comment after the last character that fits, or at any character if nothing.\n\nThe card is replaced by the new card when a layout keyword is given, even if the value is the same.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.setvalue!"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.splitcard-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{K, S, C, Any}} where {K<:AbstractString, S<:AbstractString, C<:AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"splitcard(key, value, comment, format)\n\nSplit card having long value and comment fields into multiple cards\n\nIf length of comment field is 0 because comment separator index is too large, then comment is deleted.  Decreasing comment separator index will allow inclusion of comment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.splitcard"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.parse-Tuple{Type{Card}, AbstractString, Any}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.parse(::Type{Card}, image::AbstractString, previous; strict = true, units = true)\n\nParse 80 character card image that follows the card previous, or nothing. A CONTINUE card that does not continue a string is commentary text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parse"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.parse-Tuple{Type{Card}, AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.parse(::Type{Card}, image::AbstractString; strict = true, units = true)\n\nParse 80 character card image from string buffer. A number with a lower case exponent letter (d or e) is not allowed by the standard, so it is an invalid card, unless strict is false. The units of a number, i.e., a unit string in square brackets at the beginning of the comment, are the units of the value, unless units is false or the keyword is mandatory.\n\nThe card is written as it is read, with the following exceptions, where the value is not changed: a number without an integer digit has a zero, e.g., .5 is 0.5; an exponent has at least two digits and a mantissa has one integer digit, e.g., E+007 is E+07 and 56.53E+13 is 5.653E+14; the sign of an integer zero is lost; and a number that is longer than before takes the spaces before the comment, but may move the comment, and the end of the comment of a full card is lost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parse"},{"category":"function","location":"#FITSFiles.formunit","page":"FITSFiles","text":"formunit(unit[, ufmt])\n\nReturn the ASCII string of a unit in the FITS standard (Sect. 4.3). The style of the string is ufmt, which has three characters: the separator of the units, a space, ., or *; / if a unit with a negative power follows a slash, or - if it has the negative power; and the style of a power, d for digits following the unit, ^, or * for **. The default is -d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.formunit"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.parseunit-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"parseunit(string)\n\nParse a unit string of the FITS standard (Sect. 4.3) and return the unit, or nothing if the string is not a valid unit string or has a unit that is not known. Units are multiplied by a space, ., or *, and divided by /, which may begin the string. A unit is raised to a power by an integer following the unit, by a decimal number or a ratio in parentheses following the unit, or by ** or ^ followed by an integer, a decimal number, or a ratio in parentheses. A scale factor, 10**k, 10^k, or 10+k, is followed by a unit and returns a quantity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.parseunit"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.parseunit-Tuple{Module, AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"parseunit(backend, string)\n\nParse the unit string with the units of the backend, which is the module Unitful or DynamicQuantities, the latter when it is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.parseunit"}]
}
